  "explanation": {
    "short": "When two sounds come together with no vowel between them, Hindi joins the letters into one shape: स and त make स्त (na-mas-te), क and य make क्य (kyā), स and क make स्क (skūl). The first letter often loses its vertical bar. A small hook above a letter, as in चर्च, means an r said before it.",
    "more": "A letter with a small slanted stroke (्) at its foot has lost its vowel; the next letter then joins it. You have already met these shapes in नमस्ते and क्या. Look for the join, say the first sound without a vowel, then the second."
  },
